# Closed-loop action sampling

Implement the missing conditional sampler and evaluate it through the supplied
train/final submission workflow. Each published evaluation uses source-bound
private workload manifests and independently measured calibration constants.

## Problem

A simulated robot pushes a T-shaped object into a target region. A controller
observes object and robot keypoints and generates an action sequence for the next
part of the motion. The existing controller provides a frozen learned transformer,
normalization, an observation window and receding-horizon execution. The missing
component generates normalized samples conditioned on those observations. Its
output must preserve specified entries and have the required finite shape, dtype
and device. The objective is to maximize the calibrated continuous score;
the separate criterion also measures original manipulation quality and CPU cost.
You supply only the removed conditional sampler body.

## Your workspace

Run `/frontier/task/bin/prepare_workspace.sh` to copy the retained source
from `/frontier/task/repo` to `/workspace/repo` before editing it. The only editable paths are:

```text
diffusion_policy/policy/diffusion_transformer_lowdim_policy.py
diffusion_policy/sampler_extensions/*.py
```

Only `DiffusionTransformerLowdimPolicy.conditional_sample`'s body may change in
the policy file. Its signature, constructor, other methods, imports and all
retained dependencies are locked. Helper subdirectories are allowed. The supplied
physics, reward, learned transformer, normalizer and action slicing stay fixed.

## Design space

The permitted area is observation-conditioned reverse sampling using the frozen
transformer and compatible scheduler semantics. Denoising-step allocation,
stochastic or deterministic updates and sampling-loop implementation are in scope.
The controller architecture, weights, training, environment and reward are out
of scope. Preserve the conditioning and output contract; valid alternatives do
not have to return exactly the same tensor or use exactly the same number of
denoising steps. No environment inspection, seed-specific lookup, RNG reseeding,
background work that hides cost or mutation of retained code is permitted.

## Workload

The workload runs complete Push-T episodes through the unchanged
runner with the original 300-step cap and observation/prediction/action horizons
2/10/8. An episode ends on original environment success or original truncation;
truncation is not success. Candidate and restored method receive identical
episode manifests and diffusion seeds under equal resources. Private group membership, three repetitions per condition and paired scheduling
are fixed for the published version. Development and final diffusion seeds are
independent. The workload is representative of the
specified manipulation experiment, not a table of initial states to recognize.

## Interface

The preserved entry point accepts `condition_data`, `condition_mask`, optional
`cond`, optional `generator` and scheduler keyword arguments. Return a normalized
CPU tensor matching `condition_data` in shape and dtype. All entries must be
finite. Every entry selected by `condition_mask` must equal the corresponding
entry of `condition_data`; the caller performs normalization and action slicing.

For example, in a three-entry illustration with data `[1.0, 2.0, 3.0]` and mask
`[True, False, True]`, a result `[1.0, 0.5, 3.0]` respects the masking invariant,
while `[0.0, 0.5, 3.0]` does not. This is a shape/masking illustration only;
actual workloads use the fixed sequence horizon and measure closed-loop behavior.

## Validity

Static checks apply the patch to a disposable copy, verify the original source
inventory, reject changes outside the sampler body, and check bounded helpers.
You may add at most 16 regular, non-executable Python files with at most 65536
aggregate bytes in the helper namespace. Symlinks, hard links, native extensions
and data files are forbidden. The mixed policy file is limited to 131072 bytes.
The sampler executes in a separate process through a bounded tensor protocol
inside the standard platform Verifier sandbox. It can request frozen
transformer evaluations and fresh random tensors. The protocol does not expose
model weights, environment state, private manifests or rewards; submitted code
must not read private answers or modify scoring. Trial patches, trajectories and
available logs are reviewed for concrete violations after execution.
Each prediction must invoke the
frozen model at least once and at most 200 times. Source and tensor guards do not
prove that an arbitrary method follows the permitted scientific design; that
requirement also remains subject to solution review.

## Scoring

The selected continuous formula uses original mean episode-maximum reward Q,
mean prediction latency L and median complete-workload duration T. Independent
constants Qn, Ln and Tn come from a frozen natural 20-step DDPM calibration on
the exact workload, source, runtime and hardware policy. For every workload w:

```text
G(w) = max(0, (Q(w) - Qn(w)) / (1 - Qn(w)))
u(w) = G(w) * min(Ln(w) / L(w), Tn(w) / T(w))
U = mean of u(w) over every workload, with equal workload weight
F = 100 * U / (1 + U)
```

F is the bounded display and U retains unbounded improvement. Reward at or below
the independent naive anchor has zero utility. Improving the nonlimiting cost
alone can leave utility unchanged; both cost ratios must improve to increase
their minimum. Original binary success remains a mandatory separate comparison
and absolute feedback metric; it is not mixed into the continuous formula.

The optimized comparison method is not a formula input. Its pass/fail flag does
not zero a valid continuous score. Missing or mismatched calibration and
trusted reference infrastructure are authoring errors, not invented solver
zeros. A separate stricter comparison uses every metric on every workload.

{beat_statement}

## Calibration

Calibration is bound to the exact source, runtime, hardware and private suite.
Natural 20-step baseline constants and original 100-step reference measurements
are collected separately from solver trials.
Strict timing comparisons presently have zero tolerance; a later change needs
measured justification and an explicit specification revision. Small frozen
groups can establish performance only on their declared manifest, not a general
claim of distribution-wide superiority.

## Feedback and grading

The development allowance is {max_dev_submissions} submissions, derived from
the task's single development-submission setting.
The graded suite is not the suite you receive feedback on.
The graded suite includes additional initial
conditions beyond those in feedback, and freshly reruns public conditions with
independent private diffusion seeds. This extends membership without claiming
an intentional change in the initial-condition sampling distribution.

Development feedback is limited to candidate-only reward, success, prediction
latency and complete-workload duration summaries plus aggregate criterion counts.
No private rows, seeds, withheld method values or final measurements are exposed.
Invalid or incomplete development measurements remain unavailable, not invented
zeros.

## Submission

Prepare a unified Git patch rooted at the repository root, at most 262144 bytes
and 17 changed files. Include new helper files explicitly. Empty patches and
changes outside the declared surface are rejected. For an initialized disposable
working copy, an example preparation command is:

```bash
git add -N diffusion_policy/sampler_extensions
git diff --binary > /workspace/solution.patch
```

For both train and final, the uncompressed tar contains only
`payload/solution.patch` (regular file, mode 0644). The tar must fit the 262144-byte
submission limit, including archive overhead. The supplied `make_submission.sh`
packages the patch. Submit the archive through the Candidate-owned tools:

```bash
frontier-submit train /workspace/submission.tar
frontier-submit final /workspace/submission.tar
```

Final selects your explicit submitted patch, independently of earlier train scores.

## Resources

CPU-only execution is required. Each verification uses 2 CPUs, 4096 MiB memory and
4096 MiB storage, with one Torch/BLAS thread. A complete trajectory process has
a 150-second deadline, including imports, strict model loading and cleanup;
the sampler process has a 130-second lifetime. Development and independent
final budgets are 1860 and 3720 seconds. These are conservative limits derived
from private raw measurements and complete episode counts, not measured native
runtime guarantees for arbitrary submitted methods. Work exceeding a declared
limit is invalid; final receives its independent evaluation window.

Rejected development submissions return `candidate_diagnostic`, a fixed source-policy rejection category without exception details or private measurements. Only the `conditional_sample` body may change; signature whitespace remains protected.
